d because you know  as steve said we all remember what this  was like last year and even though  there's  positive tones um at the moment around  infection numbers  we cannot uh risk you know going  backwards on this so it's  um it's quite reassuring steve take us  out  to the daily mail and their front page  at uh first reading of this headline it  does seem quite shocking pay for your  own heart  op well this is the other side  of the copic coin the waiting lists that  have  grown not just kobe but the tendency to  underfund the nhs compared  with other equivalent countries and  their health provision  the combination of the two means there  are these epic  waiting lists for non-covid treatments  so what do you do now according to the  mail those with the money are throwing  money  at the situation and spending a fortune  on things like heart hops so the  headline it's not a sort of new  government policy  go out and pay for your own heart off  but it's a consequence  of the huge demand and the lack of  capacity  and um i think the kind of lack of  capacity issue  the long waiting list is going to become  a growing  political issue over the autumn and into  next year because to meet it will  involve inevitably substantial increases  in resources and staff and it's not at  all clear  that they will be in place quickly  enough to address it  hence the front page of the mail  tomorrow  yes and some of those without the  financial means will be at a real  disadvantage with with the hard  operation costing  according to the mail on average they're  20 000 pounds  i mean hugely and i i don't think the  suggestion is that it's just people who  can afford it i think it's people who  are desperate  and that's the reason why there is this  uh surge in in private  um in private treatment i think the  issue here  again just to go back to you know the  question of booster jabs and coming out  of  uh restrictions is exactly this that we  have this huge  problem that is occurrin